	'body'        => '<h2>Which memory test should you take?</h2>
<p>Each test probes a different part of short-term memory. The <a href="/number-memory-test/">number memory test</a> is the classic digit span: a number appears for a few seconds and you type it back, one digit longer every level. Most adults manage about seven. The <a href="/chimp-test/">chimp test</a> comes from a Kyoto University study in which a chimpanzee beat students at remembering the positions of numbers on a grid; here the numbers hide when you click 1 and you tap the rest in order.</p>
<p>The <a href="/visual-memory-test/">visual memory test</a> flashes a pattern of tiles and asks you to click the ones that were lit, with the grid growing every level. The <a href="/sequence-memory-test/">sequence memory test</a> is the Simon game: tiles flash in order and you repeat the order, one step longer each time. The <a href="/verbal-memory-test/">verbal memory test</a> shows words one at a time and asks whether each one appeared earlier in the round, a test of recognition memory against a pool that keeps growing. The <a href="/memory-game/">memory game</a> is the card matching game with boards from 12 to 48 cards, scored on moves and time.</p>

<h2>What counts as a good score</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Number memory:</strong> 7 digits is average, 9-11 is above average, 12 or more is exceptional.</li>
<li><strong>Chimp test:</strong> 7-9 numbers is average, 10-13 above average, 14 or more exceptional.</li>
<li><strong>Visual memory:</strong> level 6-9 is average, 10-13 above average, 14 or more excellent.</li>
<li><strong>Sequence memory:</strong> level 6-9 is average, 10-14 above average, 15 or more excellent.</li>
<li><strong>Verbal memory:</strong> 20-50 words is average, 50-100 above average, over 100 excellent.</li>
<li><strong>Memory game:</strong> divide moves by pairs; under 1.5 is excellent, under 2 is good, 2-3 is normal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How the tests relate to each other</h2>
<p>Psychologists describe working memory as a central executive with two stores: a <strong>phonological loop</strong> for sounds and words, and a <strong>visuospatial sketchpad</strong> for images and positions. The number and verbal tests load the loop; the chimp, visual and sequence tests load the sketchpad. People often score well on one side and ordinarily on the other, which is normal. All of them share a capacity limit of a handful of chunks, so the way to score higher on any of them is the same: group items into chunks, rehearse while the information is still on screen, and give abstract items (digits, tiles) a name or a shape.</p>

<h2>Are these tests diagnostic?</h2>
<p>No. Versions of these tasks are used in clinical memory assessments, but there they are administered in a fixed way and scored against age norms as one part of a wider evaluation. Scores here depend heavily on sleep, attention, practice and the device you use. Treat them as games and as a way to compare your own sessions over time. If you are worried about your memory, talk to a doctor; a low score on a web page is not evidence of anything.</p>

